\loop defines a loop, a sequence of bars and/or other loops.
Its syntax is the following:

\loop name {\bar1 \bar2 \bar3}

The name is given without the backslash, and once the loop
is defined it is called with a backslash, like a command.
Calling it alone in a line makes it the loop to be played.
It can also be called right after its definition:

\loop name {\bar1 \bar2} \name

A loop can be written in more lines too:

\loop name {
    \bar1 \bar2
    \bar3
}

A bar or loop can be repeated with * and a number,
concatenated to its name:

\loop name {\bar1*2 \bar2 \otherloop*3}

An asterisk can also be a wild card:

\loop all {*}

creates a loop with all bars, and

\loop all {**}

with the bars of all loops, one after the other, while

\loop verses {\verse*}

includes all bars the names of which contain \verse.

Methods (after a dot):

\name.goto 3

jumps to a bar of the loop, by its index (1-based).

\name.\inst1

returns the lines of \inst1 in the bars of the loop,
separated by |.

Errors worth knowing: a name that doesn't exist, a loop
name that already exists, a bar that is linked to another
bar that doesn't follow it in the loop, and a name that is
a LiveLily command.
